<h2>How the heart-rate zone calculator works</h2><p>The percent-of-max method uses an age-predicted maximum of approximately 220 minus age. The heart-rate-reserve option first subtracts resting heart rate from that maximum and then adds resting heart rate back after applying the intensity percentage.</p>
<h2>Moderate and vigorous exercise reference ranges</h2><p>The American Heart Association describes moderate-intensity activity as roughly 50–70% of maximum heart rate and vigorous activity as roughly 70–85%, while emphasizing that age-predicted values are averages. <a href="https://www.heart.org/en/healthy-living/exercise-and-physical-activity/fitness-basics/target-heart-rates" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AHA target heart-rate guidance</a>.</p>
<h2>Why two methods give different zones</h2><p>The heart-rate-reserve method accounts for your entered resting pulse, so it usually produces different targets from a simple percentage of maximum. Neither method measures your true maximum heart rate directly.</p>
<h2>Safety limitation</h2><p>This calculator is for general exercise planning, not medical clearance. Heart conditions and some medications can alter heart-rate response. People with relevant conditions or symptoms should follow individualized clinical guidance rather than a generic zone estimate.</p>
<h2>Choose zones that match your training method</h2><p>Age-predicted maximum heart rate is a rough starting point, not an individual test. Some training plans use percentage of maximum heart rate, while others use heart-rate reserve, threshold heart rate or sport-specific testing. Medication, heat, dehydration, altitude and illness can shift heart-rate response. If precise zones matter for performance or health, use a method appropriate to the athlete and obtain medical guidance when exercise safety is uncertain.</p>
